---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { getContactLinks, siteConfig } from "../site";

const contactLinks = getContactLinks();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Juha Huotari and kotona.app: a public field notebook on data platforms, infrastructure, agent-assisted engineering, and operational systems."
  pageType="profile"
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">{siteConfig.profile.name}</h1>

    <p class="page-copy">
      I’m a solution analyst in Finland working on data platforms and
      integration-heavy systems. My background spans insurance data platforms
      and regulatory reporting in wealth management—usually where business
      rules, operational processes, and durable data have to agree.
    </p>

    <h2>This site</h2>

    <p class="page-copy">
      kotona.app is where I write down how a system works while the details are
      still available: what failed, what changed, why a design was chosen, and
      what would have to be checked before doing it again.
    </p>

    <p class="page-copy">
      Most of the notes concern data platforms, declarative infrastructure,
      contract-first software, and agent-assisted engineering. I also follow a
      few questions into energy, hardware, organizations, and the built
      environment when there is a real operating problem underneath them.
    </p>

    <p class="page-copy">
      Most of these notes ask the same practical questions: who decides, where
      the truth lives, what proves it, and how a system recovers when it changes
      or fails.
    </p>

    <h2>How to read it</h2>

    <p class="page-copy">
      <strong>Projects</strong> describe things that exist: what they do, how they
      are put together, what works today, and when I last verified it.
    </p>

    <p class="page-copy">
      <strong>Notes</strong> preserve decisions, failures, operating rules, and reference
      material worth finding later. Some state a rule I use now; others connect several
      pieces of work, test an unfinished idea, or record what happened in a project.
      The labels show which kind of note it is, how strongly I hold the claim, and
      whether it is still current.
    </p>

    <p class="page-copy">
      Explorations are worked ideas, not automatically plans, forecasts, or
      recommendations. Sometimes investigating the question is the whole point.
    </p>

    <p class="page-copy">
      The writing aims to explain real systems plainly, then draw out the lesson
      where it transfers. It also serves as retrieval material for future work.
      Some notes use a more personal or creative register when the subject
      benefits from it.
    </p>

    <h2>Agents and authorship</h2>

    <p class="page-copy">
      Agents contribute substantially to the inspection, comparison,
      implementation, and drafting behind this site. I provide the source
      material and constraints, make the corrections, verify the claims, and
      decide what is published.
    </p>

    <p class="page-copy">
      Generated output is working material. The published claim is mine.
    </p>

    <p class="page-copy">
      For a longer account of the site’s authorship and editorial process, see
      {" "}
      <a href="/notes/the-workshop-is-learning-my-accent/">
        <cite>The workshop is learning my accent</cite>
      </a>.
    </p>

    <p class="page-copy">
      This is a personal site. The views published here are my own and do not
      represent my employer.
    </p>

    {
      contactLinks.length > 0 && (
        <ul class="link-row" aria-label="Contact and external profiles">
          {contactLinks.map((link) => (
            <li>
              <a
                class="link-chip"
                href={link.href}
                rel={link.href.startsWith("http") ? "me noreferrer" : undefined}
              >
                {link.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )
    }
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
